/T (For a standard reflective filter, a high \(negative\) return loss indicates that a majority of the input signal is being transmitted through the filter, ensuring efficient transfer and minimal signal loss. A high return loss indicates a well- matched filter that effectively distinguishes between the passband and stopband. MMICs have demonstrated measured passband return losses better than 10 dB for filter designs above 100 GHz \(Figure 5\) as well as typical return losses in the 15 to 25 dB range at frequencies below 40 GHz.  Tolerance & Repeatability )
