/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (like concrete or brick is increased for high frequencies. With that said though these challenges are not insurmountable and even before 5G began to build interest a wide range of applications have utilized mmWave frequencies. Current uses of mmwave technology Communications Microwaves \(of which mmWaves are a subset\) have been widely used in point- to-point communications. An example of point to point would be in a backhaul link where microwave frequencies are used to transmit data in a straight line of site link between a cellular base station and a base station controller. Point to point at mmWave frequencies is attractive because the reduced wavelength allows conveniently-sized antennas to focus them into narrow beams, which can be aimed directly at a receiving antenna. This prevents nearby microwave equipment that use the same frequency from interfering with each other, allowing the deployment of more links in the same area. In the US, 36 GHz to 40 GHz is licensed for high-speed microwave data links, and the unlicensed 60 GHz band can be used in short range data connections. In the US, 36 GHz to 40 GHz is licensed for high-speed microwave data links, and the unlicensed 60 GHz band can be used in short range data connections. In the 60GHz unlicensed band another application is WiGig, or IEEE 802.11ad, intended to support high bandwidth gaming and 4k video streaming over WiFi. The new standard adds a new 60 GHz frequency band alongside the current 2.4 and 5 GHz offerings. )
/E (like concrete or brick is increased for high frequencies. With that said though these challenges are not insurmountable and even before 5G began to build interest a wide range of applications have utilized mmWave frequencies. Current uses of mmwave technology Communications Microwaves \(of which mmWaves are a subset\) have been widely used in point- to-point communications. An example of point to point would be in a backhaul link where microwave frequencies are used to transmit data in a straight line of site link between a cellular base station and a base station controller. Point to point at mmWave frequencies is attractive because the reduced wavelength allows conveniently-sized antennas to focus them into narrow beams, which can be aimed directly at a receiving antenna. This prevents nearby microwave equipment that use the same frequency from interfering with each other, allowing the deployment of more links in the same area. In the US, 36 GHz to 40 GHz is licensed for high-speed microwave data links, and the unlicensed 60 GHz band can be used in short range data connections. In the US, 36 GHz to 40 GHz is licensed for high-speed microwave data links, and the unlicensed 60 GHz band can be used in short range data connections. In the 60GHz unlicensed band another application is WiGig, or IEEE 802.11ad, intended to support high bandwidth gaming and 4k video streaming over WiFi. The new standard adds a new 60 GHz frequency band alongside the current 2.4 and 5 GHz offerings. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
